00:01:04.989 --> 00:01:06.010
ROB SCHMIDT: All right.

00:01:06.010 --> 00:01:09.190
So let's start with a little
bit of a definition of what

00:01:09.190 --> 00:01:10.430
we're doing.

00:01:10.430 --> 00:01:12.300
So what is the edge?

00:01:12.300 --> 00:01:14.200
It's a lot of things.

00:01:14.200 --> 00:01:17.180
It can kind of be anything
you want to define it as.

00:01:17.180 --> 00:01:19.600
But for our purposes,
we're really

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:22.360
talking about areas of
constrained compute, where

00:01:22.360 --> 00:01:24.940
you have potentially a
limited amount of resources

00:01:24.940 --> 00:01:28.469
in which you can
execute your model.

00:01:28.469 --> 00:01:30.010
So in data centers,
oftentimes people

00:01:30.010 --> 00:01:31.551
are going to be
running just straight

00:01:31.551 --> 00:01:34.660
Tensorflow on IoT devices
that sort of change things

00:01:34.660 --> 00:01:35.170
with mobile.

00:01:35.170 --> 00:01:37.810
There's lots of different
techniques and things

00:01:37.810 --> 00:01:39.984
we can use, depending
on where we're going.

00:01:39.984 --> 00:01:42.400
But the fact of the matter is
that machine learning models

00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:44.920
are going to be running
everywhere on all

00:01:44.920 --> 00:01:46.510
of these surfaces very soon.

00:01:46.510 --> 00:01:48.400
So we need to start
thinking about how

00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:52.750
we start to transform
these devices

00:01:52.750 --> 00:01:54.970
and bring the services to them.

00:01:54.970 --> 00:01:59.890
And we need to do that
for a number of reasons.

00:01:59.890 --> 00:02:01.660
Obviously, with
Google Cloud, we have

00:02:01.660 --> 00:02:03.580
lots of really
awesome Vision APIs

00:02:03.580 --> 00:02:05.920
that are very easy
to adopt and give you

00:02:05.920 --> 00:02:06.920
a fair amount of things.

00:02:06.920 --> 00:02:09.370
But that comes with a cost.

00:02:09.370 --> 00:02:12.310
So for latency
reasons, you're going

00:02:12.310 --> 00:02:14.800
to have a round trip time
trying to get anything up

00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:16.780
to those APIs or back.

00:02:16.780 --> 00:02:20.740
Bandwidth is also an issue
if you're on a mobile network

00:02:20.740 --> 00:02:25.840
or you are in an area where you
don't have a good connectivity

00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:27.100
back to the Cloud.

00:02:27.100 --> 00:02:27.790
Privacy reasons.

00:02:27.790 --> 00:02:29.415
There's reasons to
do things on devices

00:02:29.415 --> 00:02:31.640
and not ship things
up to the Cloud,

00:02:31.640 --> 00:02:34.337
particularly with things
like, for example, like very

00:02:34.337 --> 00:02:36.670
sensitive health care data
or sensor data or things that

00:02:36.670 --> 00:02:38.170
are very private.

00:02:38.170 --> 00:02:40.120
Connectivity, as we
sort of alluded to,

00:02:40.120 --> 00:02:44.350
is always going to be an issue,
especially in IoT scenarios.

00:02:44.350 --> 00:02:46.750
And obviously, execution costs.

00:02:46.750 --> 00:02:51.010
A lot of times it can be quite
expensive to hurl a lot of data

00:02:51.010 --> 00:02:54.340
and a lot of compute
up in the Cloud

00:02:54.340 --> 00:02:57.470
to get this kind of stuff.

00:02:57.470 --> 00:02:59.179
But the Cloud still has a role.

00:02:59.179 --> 00:03:01.720
And we need to think about how
we leverage the Cloud for what

00:03:01.720 --> 00:03:05.440
it's good for and how we
leverage the devices for what

00:03:05.440 --> 00:03:07.690
they are really good for.

00:03:07.690 --> 00:03:09.850
So to that end,
obviously, in the Cloud

00:03:09.850 --> 00:03:11.860
you have the computing power.

00:03:11.860 --> 00:03:14.290
Obviously we've got
GPUs and TPUs available

00:03:14.290 --> 00:03:16.810
that you can bolt on
and use to accelerate

00:03:16.810 --> 00:03:20.170
your machine learning processes
and get things done faster.

00:03:20.170 --> 00:03:22.150
And that leads right
into the velocity

00:03:22.150 --> 00:03:23.920
so you're able to move
and iterate quickly

00:03:23.920 --> 00:03:26.380
by using things
like Cloud ML engine

00:03:26.380 --> 00:03:28.040
and other tools like that.

00:03:28.040 --> 00:03:30.160
The elasticity, you
scale up when you train,

00:03:30.160 --> 00:03:33.820
and when you're done you
turn it all off so you're not

00:03:33.820 --> 00:03:35.740
having to have
deadweight resources just

00:03:35.740 --> 00:03:36.820
sitting out there.

00:03:36.820 --> 00:03:38.350
Data storage is
really another area

00:03:38.350 --> 00:03:39.610
where the Cloud
makes a lot of sense

00:03:39.610 --> 00:03:41.443
because you have to
centralize and aggregate

00:03:41.443 --> 00:03:44.310
a lot of this data in
order to treat your models.

00:03:44.310 --> 00:03:46.240
And obviously training
cost is really good

00:03:46.240 --> 00:03:47.615
if we use things
like preemptible

00:03:47.615 --> 00:03:49.910
VMs and other things.

00:03:49.910 --> 00:03:51.910
And on the
elasticity, we're then

00:03:51.910 --> 00:03:56.080
able to minimize a
lot of that stuff.

00:03:56.080 --> 00:03:58.260
So there's really
three considerations

00:03:58.260 --> 00:04:00.870
that we need to think
about when we're

00:04:00.870 --> 00:04:03.840
starting to think about running
a machine learning model

00:04:03.840 --> 00:04:05.430
at the Edge.

00:04:05.430 --> 00:04:07.830
One is the model
and binary size.

00:04:07.830 --> 00:04:11.300
Obviously a lot of
these models are

00:04:11.300 --> 00:04:12.550
going to be running in memory.

00:04:12.550 --> 00:04:14.524
So if you don't have a
lot of memory, that's

00:04:14.524 --> 00:04:16.440
something we need to
control for and make sure

00:04:16.440 --> 00:04:18.660
we get that as small as
possible so that it's

00:04:18.660 --> 00:04:21.360
able to run in those scenarios.

00:04:21.360 --> 00:04:22.800
Obviously that
also has an impact

00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:24.810
to the speed at which
it's able to run.

00:04:24.810 --> 00:04:27.000
If you're using a lot
of the memory already,

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:28.650
you're not able to
push transactions

00:04:28.650 --> 00:04:29.970
through it as fast.

00:04:29.970 --> 00:04:32.760
And it also can impact
application performance.

00:04:32.760 --> 00:04:36.480
And as we look at that, slow
performance in an application

00:04:36.480 --> 00:04:37.470
is a killer.

00:04:37.470 --> 00:04:40.350
People will not tolerate
that these days.

00:04:40.350 --> 00:04:43.337
And you have to start thinking
about your device itself

00:04:43.337 --> 00:04:43.920
and threading.

00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:46.680
So by default,
Tensorflow sets that

00:04:46.680 --> 00:04:48.690
to the default number
of cores on the device,

00:04:48.690 --> 00:04:51.510
but we need to understand
that hardware profile so we

00:04:51.510 --> 00:04:54.750
can decide, do we to
utilize more cores,

00:04:54.750 --> 00:04:57.150
do we need to utilize
more threads in order

00:04:57.150 --> 00:05:00.290
to move forward.

00:05:00.290 --> 00:05:01.450
So that's all great.

00:05:01.450 --> 00:05:04.017
We all understand
the problems faced.

00:05:04.017 --> 00:05:05.350
But how do we actually go there?

00:05:05.350 --> 00:05:08.830
And there's really four phases
that we need to think about.

00:05:08.830 --> 00:05:10.960
And there's a lot of
constraints and considerations

00:05:10.960 --> 00:05:12.334
that we need to
take into account

00:05:12.334 --> 00:05:15.760
at each one of these phases
in order to get our model out.

00:05:15.760 --> 00:05:19.240
So first we're going to talk
about building the model.

00:05:19.240 --> 00:05:22.060
Obviously we're primarily
talking about Tensorflow here,

00:05:22.060 --> 00:05:24.070
and the reason we're
talking about Tensorflow

00:05:24.070 --> 00:05:28.060
is because it's a very
flexible execution environment

00:05:28.060 --> 00:05:30.946
and training environment
for machine learning.

00:05:30.946 --> 00:05:32.320
So we're able to
actually utilize

00:05:32.320 --> 00:05:36.100
it to go straight from the
Cloud to the Edge with very

00:05:36.100 --> 00:05:39.790
little change to
the actual machinery

00:05:39.790 --> 00:05:41.890
that we're running on.

00:05:41.890 --> 00:05:44.510
But when we can start
to consider the problem

00:05:44.510 --> 00:05:46.780
and what we're doing,
we need to understand

00:05:46.780 --> 00:05:49.840
where we're going to be putting
this model at the beginning.

00:05:49.840 --> 00:05:51.880
Choices you make at
this stage of the game

00:05:51.880 --> 00:05:53.739
have a very severe
downstream impact

00:05:53.739 --> 00:05:55.030
if you're not considering them.

00:05:55.030 --> 00:05:56.830
So we want to
understand where we're

00:05:56.830 --> 00:05:57.670
going to be running this model.

00:05:57.670 --> 00:06:00.100
Is it going to be running on
a Raspberry Pi in a closet?

00:06:00.100 --> 00:06:01.660
Is it going to be
running directly

00:06:01.660 --> 00:06:04.480
on a piece of machinery?

00:06:04.480 --> 00:06:06.020
What kind of
hardware environment?

00:06:06.020 --> 00:06:07.450
What are the
latency requirements

00:06:07.450 --> 00:06:08.920
of this particular
model that we're

00:06:08.920 --> 00:06:10.960
going to be trying to run?

00:06:10.960 --> 00:06:14.270
And understand that as
we make those choices,

00:06:14.270 --> 00:06:18.440
we're going to be constrained
in several different ways.

00:06:18.440 --> 00:06:22.100
So whether it's the RAM or
the compute performance,

00:06:22.100 --> 00:06:23.530
whether it's the
size of the model

00:06:23.530 --> 00:06:26.720
or the choice of the model that
we're going to use or build,

00:06:26.720 --> 00:06:29.050
we need to understand
at that point what

00:06:29.050 --> 00:06:32.020
we're going to be doing so that
we can choose the right model

00:06:32.020 --> 00:06:33.431
and build the right model.

00:06:33.431 --> 00:06:34.930
So components at
this point, there's

00:06:34.930 --> 00:06:35.950
a lot of different
things that we use.

00:06:35.950 --> 00:06:37.450
Obviously Tensorflow
I talked about.

00:06:37.450 --> 00:06:39.520
But having Jupyter
and IPython notebooks

00:06:39.520 --> 00:06:42.845
so your team of engineers
and data scientists

00:06:42.845 --> 00:06:45.220
can iterate quickly and share
the data and get everything

00:06:45.220 --> 00:06:47.690
moving quickly is important.

00:06:47.690 --> 00:06:49.870
Kubeflow if you're
in on Kubernetes,

00:06:49.870 --> 00:06:51.550
Kubeflow is another
great pattern

00:06:51.550 --> 00:06:53.920
that people are starting
to adopt because it kind

00:06:53.920 --> 00:06:56.710
of homogeneous is a
lot of that training

00:06:56.710 --> 00:06:58.810
infrastructure and
IPython notebooks

00:06:58.810 --> 00:07:00.940
and sharing and all that stuff.

00:07:00.940 --> 00:07:04.320
Obviously the big, glaring
hole that everybody

00:07:04.320 --> 00:07:08.080
needs to think about is
training data and testing data.

00:07:08.080 --> 00:07:11.320
If you don't have enough data,
there are ways around that.

00:07:11.320 --> 00:07:14.920
But obviously we need to have
good quality data so that we're

00:07:14.920 --> 00:07:16.570
able to actually
build this model

00:07:16.570 --> 00:07:18.780
and get good
performance out of it.

00:07:18.780 --> 00:07:21.160
But in some cases where
you don't have good data,

00:07:21.160 --> 00:07:22.680
or maybe it's not
particularly good,

00:07:22.680 --> 00:07:25.771
you can start with a
pre-trained model from TF Hub,

00:07:25.771 --> 00:07:28.270
pull that down, and then you
can do a little bit of transfer

00:07:28.270 --> 00:07:30.820
learning on it to
accelerate yourself and not

00:07:30.820 --> 00:07:35.830
maybe be constrained by as
much data as you may need.

00:07:35.830 --> 00:07:38.350
So when we look at the
actual training of the model,

00:07:38.350 --> 00:07:40.391
first we have to make sure
we've got enough data,

00:07:40.391 --> 00:07:41.474
as I just said.

00:07:41.474 --> 00:07:43.390
Another constraint that
we need to think about

00:07:43.390 --> 00:07:46.210
is how fast do we actually
need to do this training?

00:07:46.210 --> 00:07:48.160
How often are we
changing the model?

00:07:48.160 --> 00:07:53.170
How often are we looking at
actually improving the model,

00:07:53.170 --> 00:07:56.170
because that has an impact, too.

00:07:56.170 --> 00:07:59.299
If you are in an IoT use
case in manufacturing,

00:07:59.299 --> 00:08:01.090
and you tell your plant
manager that you're

00:08:01.090 --> 00:08:03.506
going to have to take a whole
machine down for three hours

00:08:03.506 --> 00:08:05.560
so you can update
the sensor model,

00:08:05.560 --> 00:08:07.810
they're not going to
be happy with you.

00:08:07.810 --> 00:08:10.560
That's money just
flowing out the door.

00:08:10.560 --> 00:08:12.310
Another thing to think
about at this point

00:08:12.310 --> 00:08:14.920
is what we call quantization.

00:08:14.920 --> 00:08:16.360
Quantization is
something that you

00:08:16.360 --> 00:08:19.870
package into the actual training
execution in Tensorflow.

00:08:19.870 --> 00:08:23.380
And what quantization does is
it simply takes the graph--

00:08:23.380 --> 00:08:27.190
so Tensorflow executes in
floating point by default.

00:08:27.190 --> 00:08:32.595
But on an Edge device or on
a very constrained area where

00:08:32.595 --> 00:08:33.970
you don't have a
lot of GPUs, you

00:08:33.970 --> 00:08:36.159
don't have a lot of floating
point operations, that

00:08:36.159 --> 00:08:37.263
can be really hard to run.

00:08:37.263 --> 00:08:38.679
It's really hard
for mobile phones

00:08:38.679 --> 00:08:42.722
to run all that floating
point stuff at the moment.

00:08:42.722 --> 00:08:44.680
So what quantization does
is it takes all of it

00:08:44.680 --> 00:08:47.320
and flips it into 8-bit
integer calculation.

00:08:47.320 --> 00:08:49.100
Now there's a
consequence to that.

00:08:49.100 --> 00:08:50.683
So you're going to
get the performance

00:08:50.683 --> 00:08:53.410
and you're going to get a
lot of increase and boost,

00:08:53.410 --> 00:08:56.650
but your model is going
to be less accurate now.

00:08:56.650 --> 00:08:58.399
So when you quantize
the model, you also

00:08:58.399 --> 00:09:00.190
have to make sure that
you're understanding

00:09:00.190 --> 00:09:01.773
what that accuracy
loss is going to be

00:09:01.773 --> 00:09:05.100
and what you actually
need to be successful.

00:09:05.100 --> 00:09:06.880
Obviously, in a lot
of various industries,

00:09:06.880 --> 00:09:08.870
there's going to be
specialized hardware,

00:09:08.870 --> 00:09:09.400
as we're starting to see.

00:09:09.400 --> 00:09:11.233
We announced Edge TPUs
and things like that.

00:09:11.233 --> 00:09:14.680
So we're starting to see some
changes to how we're actually

00:09:14.680 --> 00:09:17.230
building these things.

00:09:17.230 --> 00:09:20.022
And that's an important
thing to understand here

00:09:20.022 --> 00:09:22.480
because you have to know what
you're going to be running on

00:09:22.480 --> 00:09:24.688
so that you can actually
make sure that the execution

00:09:24.688 --> 00:09:25.934
environment is good.

00:09:25.934 --> 00:09:27.850
And obviously one of the
big risks to training

00:09:27.850 --> 00:09:30.520
is always data bias.

00:09:30.520 --> 00:09:32.897
Oftentimes we bias the
data unintentionally.

00:09:32.897 --> 00:09:34.480
We walk in with the
best of intentions

00:09:34.480 --> 00:09:36.910
and wind up doing it
ourselves because we're humans

00:09:36.910 --> 00:09:38.740
and we're biased.

00:09:38.740 --> 00:09:41.192
So understanding that you
might not have all the data,

00:09:41.192 --> 00:09:43.150
or you might be excluding
certain kinds of data

00:09:43.150 --> 00:09:46.860
that have an impact to your
model, is important as well.

00:09:46.860 --> 00:09:49.750
So as we look at the
architecture of components

00:09:49.750 --> 00:09:52.141
that we would use here,
obviously Kubeflow

00:09:52.141 --> 00:09:53.890
can be a training and
an execution engine.

00:09:53.890 --> 00:09:58.170
You can use the Cloud ML engine
for fully serverless style

00:09:58.170 --> 00:10:02.630
of training and execution is
another key component, too.

00:10:02.630 --> 00:10:04.900
And obviously using TPUs
and GPUs at this point

00:10:04.900 --> 00:10:07.240
are really good
because it's going

00:10:07.240 --> 00:10:08.860
to start to shrink
that time down

00:10:08.860 --> 00:10:13.300
that you're going to need to
actually build this model out

00:10:13.300 --> 00:10:15.940
and get it trained and tested.

00:10:15.940 --> 00:10:19.910
So moving on to the
preparing the model phase.

00:10:19.910 --> 00:10:22.010
So at this stage, really,
what we're trying to do

00:10:22.010 --> 00:10:26.659
is understand that
we've got to understand

00:10:26.659 --> 00:10:28.700
what the performance
characteristics of the model

00:10:28.700 --> 00:10:30.980
are going to be.

00:10:30.980 --> 00:10:33.470
A lot of ways, things
that we can do,

00:10:33.470 --> 00:10:35.870
are built into the Tensorflow
libraries themselves.

00:10:35.870 --> 00:10:38.535
But we want to make sure that
we understand exactly what we're

00:10:38.535 --> 00:10:40.160
going to be doing
this model on and how

00:10:40.160 --> 00:10:41.910
it's going to execute
in the environment

00:10:41.910 --> 00:10:43.960
that we're going to
be running it in.

00:10:43.960 --> 00:10:45.470
So a couple of tools
that we can use

00:10:45.470 --> 00:10:46.710
are the Tensorflow profiler.

00:10:46.710 --> 00:10:47.840
This is a great
built-in tool that

00:10:47.840 --> 00:10:49.714
can give you actual
performance metrics based

00:10:49.714 --> 00:10:52.930
on the graph that comes out
of the training session.

00:10:52.930 --> 00:10:55.430
And then the other thing that
we start to look at this point

00:10:55.430 --> 00:10:58.340
is the TFLite Benchmark Library.

00:10:58.340 --> 00:11:03.020
So if you're in a mobile
or in an IoT situation,

00:11:03.020 --> 00:11:04.880
TensorflowLite is
probably the destination

00:11:04.880 --> 00:11:06.920
you're going to wind up at.

00:11:06.920 --> 00:11:08.330
It's built in.

00:11:08.330 --> 00:11:13.350
What it does is it restricts a
number of heavyweight hardcore

00:11:13.350 --> 00:11:16.010
CPU operations from the model.

00:11:16.010 --> 00:11:19.190
And it comes with a
custom protocol buffer

00:11:19.190 --> 00:11:21.380
that lets you execute--
it's specifically

00:11:21.380 --> 00:11:24.032
designed to be executed
in mobile environments.

00:11:24.032 --> 00:11:26.240
And so what you do is, after
you quantize your model,

00:11:26.240 --> 00:11:29.180
you're able to run this
converter called TOCO,

00:11:29.180 --> 00:11:32.240
and that's what turns it into
the actual Tensorflow Lite

00:11:32.240 --> 00:11:35.750
graph that you can
then take and execute.

00:11:35.750 --> 00:11:37.850
A couple of other things
to consider at this point

00:11:37.850 --> 00:11:39.260
are model encryption.

00:11:39.260 --> 00:11:41.720
Obviously a lot of these
models are running in memory,

00:11:41.720 --> 00:11:46.310
and encryption is baked into
Tensorflow or Tensorflow Lite,

00:11:46.310 --> 00:11:48.990
but it is available to build.

00:11:48.990 --> 00:11:51.940
There's a little tutorial
up on the Tensorflow website

00:11:51.940 --> 00:11:54.590
if you are concerned about
people scraping the memory

00:11:54.590 --> 00:11:57.180
and taking your model and
doing something else with it.

00:11:57.180 --> 00:11:58.190
So that's something
that you also

00:11:58.190 --> 00:11:59.870
want to consider at
this point as well.

00:12:02.486 --> 00:12:05.110
At this point, now we've got to
actually get the model running.

00:12:05.110 --> 00:12:06.693
And there's a number
of different ways

00:12:06.693 --> 00:12:08.474
we could actually
approach this situation.

00:12:08.474 --> 00:12:10.140
Obviously we could
run it in containers.

00:12:10.140 --> 00:12:12.360
We could run natively
on Android or iOS,

00:12:12.360 --> 00:12:15.000
package it right into our app.

00:12:15.000 --> 00:12:16.200
Do we have Edge TPUs?

00:12:16.200 --> 00:12:17.784
Are we using Android IoT?

00:12:17.784 --> 00:12:19.950
We could have Kubeflow
deployed to an Edge location,

00:12:19.950 --> 00:12:23.400
like, for example, if you have
a fleet of different service

00:12:23.400 --> 00:12:26.670
centers with small data centers
inside of them, like three

00:12:26.670 --> 00:12:30.587
or four NUCs from Intel, you
could deploy Kubeflow there.

00:12:30.587 --> 00:12:32.670
And then you have a nice
really end-to-end service

00:12:32.670 --> 00:12:33.660
at that point.

00:12:33.660 --> 00:12:36.450
But you kind of need to
take into account everything

00:12:36.450 --> 00:12:38.580
that you've got rolling
in your data centers

00:12:38.580 --> 00:12:40.710
and where you're
going to be, and think

00:12:40.710 --> 00:12:42.810
about how often, at
this point in time,

00:12:42.810 --> 00:12:44.760
you're going to be
training that model.

00:12:44.760 --> 00:12:46.895
So if you package
everything into an app

00:12:46.895 --> 00:12:48.770
and you retrain your
model, well, guess what?

00:12:48.770 --> 00:12:51.150
Now you've got to push the
new app out to the app store.

00:12:51.150 --> 00:12:52.983
People got to go and
download and update it.

00:12:52.983 --> 00:12:56.040
So there's often times you
need to think about, OK,

00:12:56.040 --> 00:12:57.900
how am I going to do
this so that I'm giving

00:12:57.900 --> 00:13:00.360
my users the best experience,
given the fact that I'm

00:13:00.360 --> 00:13:01.984
probably going to be
training the model

00:13:01.984 --> 00:13:04.590
more frequently than I'm going
to be updating the application,

00:13:04.590 --> 00:13:07.540
because that's going to be a
key thing that happens here.

00:13:07.540 --> 00:13:10.080
The thing about machine learning
is it doesn't really stop.

00:13:10.080 --> 00:13:12.555
It has to keep going.

00:13:12.555 --> 00:13:14.930
So with that, I would like to
bring Ryan back up on stage

00:13:14.930 --> 00:13:18.380
to talk about the journey
that his team has gone through

00:13:18.380 --> 00:13:21.830
to try to execute a very similar
vision as the one we just

00:13:21.830 --> 00:13:23.410
talked about.

00:13:23.410 --> 00:13:25.820
RYAN MCGUIRE: Thanks, Rob.

00:13:25.820 --> 00:13:28.070
So we're going to talk a
little bit about an example

00:13:28.070 --> 00:13:31.790
today, about Koko.

00:13:31.790 --> 00:13:33.860
For those of you
who know Koko, Koko

00:13:33.860 --> 00:13:37.040
was a gorilla who
learned sign language.

00:13:37.040 --> 00:13:40.160
Actually, Koko was born
here in San Francisco

00:13:40.160 --> 00:13:42.260
just down the road at the zoo.

00:13:42.260 --> 00:13:45.530
And over a period of time,
she learned sign language,

00:13:45.530 --> 00:13:47.160
was able to communicate.

00:13:47.160 --> 00:13:48.980
So we're going to
use that as a model

00:13:48.980 --> 00:13:52.550
today to demonstrate what
Rob talked about about some

00:13:52.550 --> 00:13:54.440
of those flows.

00:13:54.440 --> 00:13:57.140
At Solstice, our
mission is to prove

00:13:57.140 --> 00:13:59.060
what humanity is capable of.

00:13:59.060 --> 00:14:02.420
And what a better way to take
machine learning, computer

00:14:02.420 --> 00:14:05.390
vision, neural networks,
and an experience

00:14:05.390 --> 00:14:10.280
to help those in need to prove
what humanity is capable of.

00:14:10.280 --> 00:14:13.190
So we're going to walk through
this slide a little bit

00:14:13.190 --> 00:14:15.890
and talk about the steps that
Rob talked about, step stop

00:14:15.890 --> 00:14:18.650
being building your
model, using the Cloud

00:14:18.650 --> 00:14:21.470
and the power of the
Cloud to do that.

00:14:21.470 --> 00:14:24.890
Here we had a series
of images that we

00:14:24.890 --> 00:14:27.050
use to train the model
to make sure that we

00:14:27.050 --> 00:14:28.370
selected the right model.

00:14:28.370 --> 00:14:31.630
As Rob had talked about with the
deployment model of the Edge,

00:14:31.630 --> 00:14:33.740
we want to give
consideration to the things

00:14:33.740 --> 00:14:37.850
that we might not
have thought through.

00:14:37.850 --> 00:14:42.140
So what operations could
go through a Tensor Lite

00:14:42.140 --> 00:14:44.180
conversion, and how
would we do that?

00:14:44.180 --> 00:14:47.132
Should we use an
object recognition

00:14:47.132 --> 00:14:49.340
that's already trained, or
should we train ourselves?

00:14:49.340 --> 00:14:50.540
Should we use SSD?

00:14:50.540 --> 00:14:52.274
Should we use VGG?

00:14:52.274 --> 00:14:53.190
Those types of things.

00:14:53.190 --> 00:14:55.430
So in this phase, we
started to work out

00:14:55.430 --> 00:14:58.040
a lot of those types of things
using the power of the Cloud

00:14:58.040 --> 00:15:02.949
with Google Compute Engine
to do those types of things.

00:15:02.949 --> 00:15:05.240
Really important in this
space is to have your training

00:15:05.240 --> 00:15:07.040
set and your test
set aligned up.

00:15:07.040 --> 00:15:08.960
So I'll show a demo
in a little bit

00:15:08.960 --> 00:15:12.500
about using a
sparsely trained set.

00:15:12.500 --> 00:15:16.310
We'll call her Koko Jr. She
only knows three letters.

00:15:16.310 --> 00:15:20.210
And then we'll show kind of
a demo of Koko Senior being

00:15:20.210 --> 00:15:23.000
trained on a full set
of data, different hand

00:15:23.000 --> 00:15:25.600
gestures, different
colors, left and right

00:15:25.600 --> 00:15:27.230
and those types
of things, to have

00:15:27.230 --> 00:15:30.500
a more rich and robust model.

00:15:30.500 --> 00:15:33.445
Once you have that model
and you've got it built,

00:15:33.445 --> 00:15:34.320
you want to train it.

00:15:34.320 --> 00:15:36.860
You want to set some
validation points.

00:15:36.860 --> 00:15:38.840
Am I comfortable
at 50% accuracy?

00:15:38.840 --> 00:15:40.400
Do I need a 90% accuracy?

00:15:40.400 --> 00:15:42.320
How much coverage do I want?

00:15:42.320 --> 00:15:45.320
So we'll talk a little bit
about how we did that there.

00:15:45.320 --> 00:15:48.170
And then this whole quantize.

00:15:48.170 --> 00:15:49.610
It's a cool word, right?

00:15:49.610 --> 00:15:51.020
The Edge, quantize.

00:15:51.020 --> 00:15:53.960
This is where you now get to
take the power of the Cloud

00:15:53.960 --> 00:15:55.700
and compact it.

00:15:55.700 --> 00:15:59.180
A whole world in a
teeny itty bitty space.

00:15:59.180 --> 00:16:01.970
So putting it out to the Edge,
so getting ready to do that.

00:16:01.970 --> 00:16:04.520
So we use quantization to
do that conversion down

00:16:04.520 --> 00:16:06.620
to Tensorflow Lite.

00:16:06.620 --> 00:16:10.380
And then we use traditional
means of CICD pipelines.

00:16:10.380 --> 00:16:13.250
So we're constantly training
the model in the Cloud

00:16:13.250 --> 00:16:16.017
with new imagery and
data that we have.

00:16:16.017 --> 00:16:17.600
And we have a CICD
pipeline that we'll

00:16:17.600 --> 00:16:19.370
demonstrate in the
application where

00:16:19.370 --> 00:16:22.010
we're able to make the
switch between Koko Jr.

00:16:22.010 --> 00:16:25.340
And Koko Sr. in near
real time, which

00:16:25.340 --> 00:16:28.680
is really important to
the user experience.

00:16:28.680 --> 00:16:29.870
Does that make sense?

00:16:29.870 --> 00:16:30.650
Yeah?

00:16:30.650 --> 00:16:33.230
Who wants to see Koko?

00:16:33.230 --> 00:16:35.150
Before we do, I want
to talk a little bit

00:16:35.150 --> 00:16:37.590
about the differences here.

00:16:37.590 --> 00:16:40.370
So you can see the top
track, we have Koko Jr.,

00:16:40.370 --> 00:16:43.520
who's only trained on A
and B with a minimal set

00:16:43.520 --> 00:16:45.110
of observations.

00:16:45.110 --> 00:16:47.570
That model is
available in a version

00:16:47.570 --> 00:16:49.850
in our continuous
integration environment

00:16:49.850 --> 00:16:53.720
where we can pull that down
from our storage bucket.

00:16:53.720 --> 00:16:56.780
On the bottom, we've taking
more of a robust approach using

00:16:56.780 --> 00:17:01.880
the power of Auto ML
engine, imagery, and data

00:17:01.880 --> 00:17:07.699
to really train Koko Sr. To
know the soup to nuts alphabet

00:17:07.699 --> 00:17:09.240
and interfacing with
the application.

00:17:09.240 --> 00:17:12.510
So with that said,
we're going to bring up

00:17:12.510 --> 00:17:16.619
Koko Jr. If you could
switch over the displays.

00:17:16.619 --> 00:17:22.470
So here we have an Android
app displayed here on screen.

00:17:22.470 --> 00:17:25.770
Image recognition
and cameras on.

00:17:25.770 --> 00:17:28.530
You can see the image
process over here,

00:17:28.530 --> 00:17:33.150
which will do motion detection
once we put up a hand signal.

00:17:33.150 --> 00:17:36.310
But as demos go, let's
see if she's working.

00:17:45.140 --> 00:17:46.610
Ah, she worked.

00:17:46.610 --> 00:17:51.170
She knows A. Yay.

00:17:51.170 --> 00:17:58.210
However, if we go to H,
which is this signal,

00:17:58.210 --> 00:18:00.460
no letter detected.

00:18:00.460 --> 00:18:02.925
But to prove out
that she knows B,

00:18:02.925 --> 00:18:05.790
we can go ahead and
put up the B signal.

00:18:05.790 --> 00:18:08.770
So she knows B,
but she's only 61%

00:18:08.770 --> 00:18:11.530
certain that it's
a B. So now what

00:18:11.530 --> 00:18:13.600
we're going to do is
we're going to pull down,

00:18:13.600 --> 00:18:17.410
through the CICD pipeline that
we have available with Koko Sr.

00:18:17.410 --> 00:18:18.310
Trained.

00:18:18.310 --> 00:18:22.000
So we've got these buttons
that basically toggle versions

00:18:22.000 --> 00:18:25.270
of the model back and forth
inside of the application

00:18:25.270 --> 00:18:27.430
so that we get the
latest and greatest.

00:18:27.430 --> 00:18:31.210
So we're going to go ahead
and update here to Koko Sr.,

00:18:31.210 --> 00:18:34.820
and as you see, you can see,
Koko Sr. has now taken over.

00:18:34.820 --> 00:18:37.730
But nothing's really
changed from the experience.

00:18:37.730 --> 00:18:38.560
So we can go ahead.

00:18:41.840 --> 00:19:01.160
OK, so she knows A. But
let's see if she knows H. O.

00:19:01.160 --> 00:19:04.070
So you can see that
the power of having

00:19:04.070 --> 00:19:06.440
data in a different
training set brings you

00:19:06.440 --> 00:19:08.870
to the next level of what
you're capable of doing.

00:19:08.870 --> 00:19:10.550
I could show you
right hand signals.

00:19:10.550 --> 00:19:12.770
We could have a small
children's hand,

00:19:12.770 --> 00:19:14.480
a woman's hand, a man's hand.

00:19:14.480 --> 00:19:16.850
All of these are different
signals into your environment

00:19:16.850 --> 00:19:19.910
when you're thinking about the
model that you're creating.

00:19:19.910 --> 00:19:23.910
So your test inputs, your
data set are very important.

00:19:23.910 --> 00:19:26.480
But let's envision for a
second that we took this

00:19:26.480 --> 00:19:32.180
to the next level and we
didn't have that training set.

00:19:32.180 --> 00:19:34.880
You could have the power
of a continuous learning

00:19:34.880 --> 00:19:39.020
cycle with Koko that could
capture, go into training mode,

00:19:39.020 --> 00:19:42.200
and now it's capturing images
and you're allowing your user

00:19:42.200 --> 00:19:44.750
population to feed
back into that loop,

00:19:44.750 --> 00:19:46.640
creating your training set.

00:19:46.640 --> 00:19:49.509
You're not going to run
that locally at the Edge.

00:19:49.509 --> 00:19:51.800
You want that information to
go back up into the Cloud.

00:19:51.800 --> 00:19:54.560
And we're going to talk
about it a bit in a second.

00:19:54.560 --> 00:19:56.870
I'm going to call
Rob back up here.

00:19:56.870 --> 00:19:58.749
If we could switch
back the screen.

00:20:03.740 --> 00:20:05.130
Gotta click this.

00:20:05.130 --> 00:20:10.800
So Rob, is this how it works in
the real world today with Koko?

00:20:10.800 --> 00:20:11.850
ROB SCHMIDT: Yeah.

00:20:11.850 --> 00:20:16.020
But truly, that's
only half the story.

00:20:16.020 --> 00:20:18.300
As we think about this,
at this point it's like,

00:20:18.300 --> 00:20:21.300
congratulations, we've
deployed a model.

00:20:21.300 --> 00:20:23.590
Like yay.

00:20:23.590 --> 00:20:24.490
Everybody's excited.

00:20:24.490 --> 00:20:25.590
But there's so much
more we actually

00:20:25.590 --> 00:20:26.631
have to do at this point.

00:20:26.631 --> 00:20:28.169
And as Ryan alluded
to, we've got

00:20:28.169 --> 00:20:29.460
to get feedback from the users.

00:20:29.460 --> 00:20:30.793
We've got to aggregate the data.

00:20:30.793 --> 00:20:32.320
We've got to organize the data.

00:20:32.320 --> 00:20:33.570
We've got to update the model.

00:20:33.570 --> 00:20:36.090
We've got to keep going
through this cycle onward

00:20:36.090 --> 00:20:38.150
and onward and onward.

00:20:38.150 --> 00:20:40.650
So a couple of things that I
really liked about the approach

00:20:40.650 --> 00:20:43.260
that Solstice took in
this model was they

00:20:43.260 --> 00:20:46.380
decoupled the app execution
from the actual model

00:20:46.380 --> 00:20:48.430
graph, which is pretty slick.

00:20:48.430 --> 00:20:50.460
So they were able to
deploy that out to a CDN,

00:20:50.460 --> 00:20:53.730
basically, and have it going
back and forth right there

00:20:53.730 --> 00:20:56.130
without having to
update the app itself.

00:20:56.130 --> 00:20:58.895
So putting that logic into the
application was pretty smooth.

00:20:58.895 --> 00:21:00.270
And I think that's
a good pattern

00:21:00.270 --> 00:21:03.750
that you're going to start to
see people adopt going forward.

00:21:03.750 --> 00:21:04.542
RYAN MCGUIRE: Yeah.

00:21:04.542 --> 00:21:06.291
And I think it's a
really important thing.

00:21:06.291 --> 00:21:08.130
You talked about
something that all of us

00:21:08.130 --> 00:21:10.950
that are intimately familiar
with, the software engineering

00:21:10.950 --> 00:21:12.480
practices.

00:21:12.480 --> 00:21:13.940
That cycle is never done.

00:21:13.940 --> 00:21:15.570
You never finish an application.

00:21:15.570 --> 00:21:17.550
You're always
constantly grooming it.

00:21:17.550 --> 00:21:19.980
So at Solstice we've
adopted an approach called

00:21:19.980 --> 00:21:23.190
continuous learning, which
is a mix of agile principles,

00:21:23.190 --> 00:21:26.294
reinforced learning, supervised
learning, which we take it

00:21:26.294 --> 00:21:27.960
through a cycle very
much like we talked

00:21:27.960 --> 00:21:30.060
about today, and in
that prior slide,

00:21:30.060 --> 00:21:32.910
going from selecting your
model and experimenting,

00:21:32.910 --> 00:21:35.270
training your model, the
initial flow of that,

00:21:35.270 --> 00:21:37.450
evaluating what are your
criteria for getting

00:21:37.450 --> 00:21:39.630
an out the door, and
going through each one

00:21:39.630 --> 00:21:40.410
of these steps.

00:21:40.410 --> 00:21:41.520
But it's evergreen.

00:21:41.520 --> 00:21:42.730
It's never stopping.

00:21:42.730 --> 00:21:46.520
We're using modern software
engineering principles,

00:21:46.520 --> 00:21:50.850
CICD process, maybe
some A/B testing in here

00:21:50.850 --> 00:21:52.710
to put our model
out into production

00:21:52.710 --> 00:21:53.740
and get our feedback.

00:21:53.740 --> 00:21:56.070
So you can envision
an NLP chatbot

00:21:56.070 --> 00:22:00.720
that is making a recommendation
for a particular purchase order

00:22:00.720 --> 00:22:02.580
and asking for validation
from the end user.

00:22:02.580 --> 00:22:03.720
Was that right?

00:22:03.720 --> 00:22:05.670
And then that flowing
back into the process

00:22:05.670 --> 00:22:08.550
so that we can elevate the
capability of our machine

00:22:08.550 --> 00:22:10.800
learning models in
a commercialization

00:22:10.800 --> 00:22:15.090
to go from just reasoning for
a human to actually helping

00:22:15.090 --> 00:22:16.800
make decisions.

00:22:16.800 --> 00:22:18.510
So one of the things
that we'd like to do

00:22:18.510 --> 00:22:21.540
is just kind of walk through,
between Google and Solstice,

00:22:21.540 --> 00:22:25.140
put together this model of what
an ideal state architecture is

00:22:25.140 --> 00:22:27.510
and how that might fit into
a model to give you guys

00:22:27.510 --> 00:22:31.494
the tools, as you walk away from
this conference, this meeting,

00:22:31.494 --> 00:22:33.660
and you think about how
you're going to take forward

00:22:33.660 --> 00:22:37.120
what you've learned here today
and apply it to your business.

00:22:37.120 --> 00:22:40.590
So we'll start with the
Experiment and Train section.

00:22:40.590 --> 00:22:44.220
Here, using GCP, we've
lined up the ability

00:22:44.220 --> 00:22:47.850
to use BigQuery, Bigtable
and other Cloud storage

00:22:47.850 --> 00:22:50.130
as kind of that
foundation data layer.

00:22:50.130 --> 00:22:54.510
And we'll talk about layers,
how that true feedback

00:22:54.510 --> 00:22:56.160
data flows into it.

00:22:56.160 --> 00:22:58.800
But you can see you've got
your dev cluster and your prod

00:22:58.800 --> 00:23:01.890
models living side by
side, creating that trained

00:23:01.890 --> 00:23:05.730
model that's constantly
evaluating new sets of data

00:23:05.730 --> 00:23:08.010
and new points of
data inside of that.

00:23:08.010 --> 00:23:09.310
Anything to add there, Rob?

00:23:09.310 --> 00:23:10.060
ROB SCHMIDT: Yeah.

00:23:10.060 --> 00:23:12.060
I think it's an
important piece that you

00:23:12.060 --> 00:23:14.910
need to have that I think people
don't think about as they're

00:23:14.910 --> 00:23:17.370
going through this, is curating
your data and making sure

00:23:17.370 --> 00:23:20.580
that you have a consistent
environment for your teams

00:23:20.580 --> 00:23:23.820
to actually build and
experiment with these models on,

00:23:23.820 --> 00:23:26.460
and making sure that you're
doing that in a safe way,

00:23:26.460 --> 00:23:29.327
making sure that everybody
has access to what they need,

00:23:29.327 --> 00:23:31.410
making sure you're respecting
user privacy and all

00:23:31.410 --> 00:23:33.857
the other concerns that we
have in software development.

00:23:33.857 --> 00:23:35.440
And then you give
them the opportunity

00:23:35.440 --> 00:23:38.820
to just go right from that
spot to Cloud machine learning

00:23:38.820 --> 00:23:41.070
and do the actual
training execution.

00:23:41.070 --> 00:23:43.560
So you're basically
unconstraining them now

00:23:43.560 --> 00:23:45.150
from resource usage.

00:23:45.150 --> 00:23:46.740
And that's, I think,
the ideal states

00:23:46.740 --> 00:23:48.780
because it's going to
let people move quickly

00:23:48.780 --> 00:23:52.009
and actually start to execute
these patterns rapidly.

00:23:52.009 --> 00:23:52.800
RYAN MCGUIRE: Yeah.

00:23:52.800 --> 00:23:53.841
No, I think that's great.

00:23:53.841 --> 00:23:57.610
And this kind of blueprint
gives a, well, where do I start

00:23:57.610 --> 00:24:00.820
and when do I end, and
how do I start it again.

00:24:00.820 --> 00:24:02.910
And so it's a really
good model to it.

00:24:02.910 --> 00:24:05.980
So stepping out of that, it's
into the Evaluation and Deploy

00:24:05.980 --> 00:24:06.690
stage.

00:24:06.690 --> 00:24:09.690
You can take this for
what it is at this level

00:24:09.690 --> 00:24:11.320
or you can even
go further detail.

00:24:11.320 --> 00:24:13.700
So put in whatever
continuous integrations,

00:24:13.700 --> 00:24:17.460
CICD tool of your
choice in this spot,

00:24:17.460 --> 00:24:20.880
again, using that Kubeflow
deploy to the Edge,

00:24:20.880 --> 00:24:23.490
containerizing package,
doing the TFLite,

00:24:23.490 --> 00:24:26.310
using Spinnaker to get
it to the Edge device.

00:24:26.310 --> 00:24:28.770
But inside of this you could
get more sophisticated.

00:24:28.770 --> 00:24:33.810
Say you had a confusion
matrix that you wanted 90%

00:24:33.810 --> 00:24:35.340
along your confusion matrix.

00:24:35.340 --> 00:24:38.010
You want your ROC curve to
be these types of things

00:24:38.010 --> 00:24:39.840
in your continuous
integration environment,

00:24:39.840 --> 00:24:42.210
like you do today with
your web applications,

00:24:42.210 --> 00:24:44.880
you could set a
threshold for coverage.

00:24:44.880 --> 00:24:47.850
These models are set to have
these types of facilities

00:24:47.850 --> 00:24:50.040
to be able to measure
the validation of it.

00:24:50.040 --> 00:24:52.620
Your deployment here
doesn't necessarily

00:24:52.620 --> 00:24:56.070
have to overlay the
production model that's there.

00:24:56.070 --> 00:24:58.920
You can put that behind
an A/B testing platform.

00:24:58.920 --> 00:25:03.190
Say 80% of your routes are
going to go to the old model

00:25:03.190 --> 00:25:05.910
while 20% are going to
the new, so you can really

00:25:05.910 --> 00:25:08.050
measure the value of
those types of things.

00:25:08.050 --> 00:25:09.591
This is the type of
space where we're

00:25:09.591 --> 00:25:12.120
taking the history of what
we've done as software engineers

00:25:12.120 --> 00:25:14.670
and product folks and
bringing that into the model

00:25:14.670 --> 00:25:16.832
and integrate it into
your application.

00:25:16.832 --> 00:25:18.040
What do you think about that?

00:25:18.040 --> 00:25:18.790
ROB SCHMIDT: Yeah.

00:25:18.790 --> 00:25:22.440
No, it's absolutely something
I think needs to happen.

00:25:22.440 --> 00:25:24.824
I think people need
to start taking

00:25:24.824 --> 00:25:27.240
a lot of the things that's
painful about machine learning,

00:25:27.240 --> 00:25:29.640
like the performance
testing, the regression

00:25:29.640 --> 00:25:32.370
testing, figuring out what the
limits are and all that stuff,

00:25:32.370 --> 00:25:34.360
and automate it away.

00:25:34.360 --> 00:25:36.630
We've done that with
regular app deployment.

00:25:36.630 --> 00:25:38.670
We need to do it in
machine learning now

00:25:38.670 --> 00:25:41.340
because that's the thing that's
going to start to let us move

00:25:41.340 --> 00:25:42.756
faster, and it's
also going to let

00:25:42.756 --> 00:25:45.150
us start to put controls and
rigor around the testing.

00:25:45.150 --> 00:25:47.954
Rather than relying on somebody
manually rigging up a test,

00:25:47.954 --> 00:25:50.370
we're going to be able to make
it a piece of the pipeline,

00:25:50.370 --> 00:25:52.570
and it's just going to happen.

00:25:52.570 --> 00:25:55.620
Another thing to talk about that
this model sort of highlights

00:25:55.620 --> 00:25:57.750
is this concept of
localized training.

00:25:57.750 --> 00:25:59.409
And one of the things--

00:25:59.409 --> 00:26:00.950
this is really where
Kubeflow shines,

00:26:00.950 --> 00:26:03.810
this is the thing that
Kubeflow is awesome at.

00:26:03.810 --> 00:26:05.659
So one of the things
that's super important

00:26:05.659 --> 00:26:07.200
is, when we look at
the Cloud, you're

00:26:07.200 --> 00:26:10.230
going to be training against
all of your aggregate data

00:26:10.230 --> 00:26:13.920
out there.

00:26:13.920 --> 00:26:15.540
But when we look at
certain scenarios

00:26:15.540 --> 00:26:18.826
like weather forecasting--

00:26:18.826 --> 00:26:20.700
RYAN MCGUIRE: Maybe like
rain down in Africa?

00:26:20.700 --> 00:26:21.530
ROB SCHMIDT: Potentially.

00:26:21.530 --> 00:26:22.140
RYAN MCGUIRE: Potentially.

00:26:22.140 --> 00:26:24.540
ROB SCHMIDT: I'll bust the
model and then hopefully we

00:26:24.540 --> 00:26:26.547
can get it going.

00:26:26.547 --> 00:26:27.630
But it's important, right?

00:26:27.630 --> 00:26:29.390
There's going to be
regional differentiation,

00:26:29.390 --> 00:26:30.790
there's going to be
different scenarios,

00:26:30.790 --> 00:26:32.998
there's going to be different
conditions at the Edge.

00:26:32.998 --> 00:26:35.100
And you might want
to tune a model

00:26:35.100 --> 00:26:38.720
at the Edge in certain
scenarios to fit this case.

00:26:38.720 --> 00:26:41.160
So obviously I spend
my time in retail.

00:26:41.160 --> 00:26:44.160
A very classic case of this is
demand forecasting in stores.

00:26:44.160 --> 00:26:47.550
It's very easy to forecast
demand on an e-commerce site

00:26:47.550 --> 00:26:50.820
because it's all coming
through one channel.

00:26:50.820 --> 00:26:53.370
When I look at a store, a
store in California versus av

00:26:53.370 --> 00:26:56.490
store in Florida
versus a store in Maine

00:26:56.490 --> 00:26:59.700
versus a store in Texas, all
going to have different demand

00:26:59.700 --> 00:27:00.850
and supply costs.

00:27:00.850 --> 00:27:02.700
So as we start to
optimize the supply chain

00:27:02.700 --> 00:27:04.440
and make those
predictions, we probably

00:27:04.440 --> 00:27:07.740
want to start training on all
of our aggregate order data

00:27:07.740 --> 00:27:10.660
across all of our
stores figure that out.

00:27:10.660 --> 00:27:13.526
Then we deploy it locally
to the store and train

00:27:13.526 --> 00:27:15.150
against the transaction
log that that's

00:27:15.150 --> 00:27:17.640
coming in from everything
that everyone is buying.

00:27:17.640 --> 00:27:20.622
And that localizes it because
we might get bad predictions

00:27:20.622 --> 00:27:22.080
if we're looking
at the whole store

00:27:22.080 --> 00:27:24.630
because all of our
transactions aren't necessarily

00:27:24.630 --> 00:27:27.660
representative of what's going
on in each individual store.

00:27:27.660 --> 00:27:28.451
RYAN MCGUIRE: Yeah.

00:27:28.451 --> 00:27:31.440
It's that continuous
learning piece and taking

00:27:31.440 --> 00:27:33.954
the localization factor to it.

00:27:33.954 --> 00:27:35.370
A majority of the
models-- like we

00:27:35.370 --> 00:27:42.060
use an object neural network for
animals that have four classes.

00:27:42.060 --> 00:27:43.810
And that's where we
started Koko from.

00:27:43.810 --> 00:27:46.590
And we transitioned that
to learn the alphabet.

00:27:46.590 --> 00:27:48.960
Same type of thing in store.

00:27:48.960 --> 00:27:50.640
You have different
parameters you

00:27:50.640 --> 00:27:51.970
can tweak down at that level.

00:27:51.970 --> 00:27:53.592
But the base is always the same.

00:27:53.592 --> 00:27:54.300
ROB SCHMIDT: Yep.

00:27:54.300 --> 00:27:54.480
Yep.

00:27:54.480 --> 00:27:56.938
You're trained on there and
then just executing another set

00:27:56.938 --> 00:28:00.390
of training down at the Edge.

00:28:00.390 --> 00:28:02.400
RYAN MCGUIRE: The
last piece, I think,

00:28:02.400 --> 00:28:04.370
is one of the most
important factors

00:28:04.370 --> 00:28:07.710
as we think about the
maturity of models

00:28:07.710 --> 00:28:09.940
in the commercialization space.

00:28:09.940 --> 00:28:12.030
So how do you get a
model to be better?

00:28:12.030 --> 00:28:14.110
How do you take it
to the next level?

00:28:14.110 --> 00:28:20.040
How do you elevate it from
an MVP to a Version 2.0?

00:28:20.040 --> 00:28:26.850
We like to talk about models and
AI going from an infancy state

00:28:26.850 --> 00:28:29.600
to a reasoning state, and
then ultimately moving

00:28:29.600 --> 00:28:33.390
to a decisioning state
so that we can augment

00:28:33.390 --> 00:28:37.530
the human element so that we
can help the human get better

00:28:37.530 --> 00:28:41.040
and ultimately make those
decisions on their behalf

00:28:41.040 --> 00:28:42.870
with a high degree of certainty.

00:28:42.870 --> 00:28:45.180
Different businesses have
different risk thresholds

00:28:45.180 --> 00:28:46.800
and tolerances.

00:28:46.800 --> 00:28:50.100
But in our experience we don't
see those models kind of move

00:28:50.100 --> 00:28:52.200
forward without
taking this feedback

00:28:52.200 --> 00:28:56.370
loop or additional data to
adjust in the marketplace.

00:28:56.370 --> 00:28:58.200
So this is a really
important component

00:28:58.200 --> 00:28:59.700
as you think about
your architecture

00:28:59.700 --> 00:29:01.760
and you think about your
deployment of models,

00:29:01.760 --> 00:29:03.840
is how are you capturing
the customer sentiment?

00:29:03.840 --> 00:29:07.890
How are you ensuring that
what you intend to happen

00:29:07.890 --> 00:29:09.530
is happening?

00:29:09.530 --> 00:29:11.970
Compliance and
regulation, learning

00:29:11.970 --> 00:29:15.720
about how did you arrive
at a particular conclusion,

00:29:15.720 --> 00:29:18.450
and what would you do next
time when you're there?

00:29:18.450 --> 00:29:19.890
So it's a really
important event.

00:29:19.890 --> 00:29:22.780
And making that loop back
into the Cloud, where

00:29:22.780 --> 00:29:25.020
you're actually feeding
that event data back

00:29:25.020 --> 00:29:26.060
into your training set.

00:29:26.060 --> 00:29:27.060
ROB SCHMIDT: Yeah, 100%.

00:29:27.060 --> 00:29:30.642
And even then, this also
feeds the automation, more.

00:29:30.642 --> 00:29:32.100
Once we build the
model and we have

00:29:32.100 --> 00:29:34.990
that object and that artifact,
we might make tweaks to it,

00:29:34.990 --> 00:29:37.230
we might change things for
performance reasons, lots

00:29:37.230 --> 00:29:38.680
of little things like that.

00:29:38.680 --> 00:29:40.350
But really, the thing we're
going to be constantly doing

00:29:40.350 --> 00:29:42.570
is training it and training
it again and training it again

00:29:42.570 --> 00:29:43.486
and training it again.

00:29:43.486 --> 00:29:45.460
So if you build this
pipeline like this,

00:29:45.460 --> 00:29:47.950
you're able to automate
all of that stuff away.

00:29:47.950 --> 00:29:51.360
So now your high value,
hard to find, hard

00:29:51.360 --> 00:29:54.690
to hire data scientists and
machine learning experts

00:29:54.690 --> 00:29:56.790
are able to keep going
and working on new stuff

00:29:56.790 --> 00:30:00.090
rather than having to continue
to maintain a model over

00:30:00.090 --> 00:30:02.370
and over and over again by hand.

00:30:02.370 --> 00:30:06.470
So it's very much
reflective of the changes

00:30:06.470 --> 00:30:09.380
we've seen in the industry
to this point, especially

00:30:09.380 --> 00:30:12.920
as we look at app
development and the way

00:30:12.920 --> 00:30:15.890
that space has evolved over
the past five or six years,

00:30:15.890 --> 00:30:18.350
and bringing that, now,
that same sort of rigor

00:30:18.350 --> 00:30:20.119
and decision to
machine learning.

00:30:20.119 --> 00:30:21.410
RYAN MCGUIRE: No, that's great.

00:30:21.410 --> 00:30:25.752
Well, thanks for allowing
us to have that opportunity

00:30:25.752 --> 00:30:26.960
come up here and talk to you.

00:30:26.960 --> 00:30:30.050
Hopefully you guys have
walked away with a little bit

00:30:30.050 --> 00:30:32.640
more insight and knowledge
as you kind OF wine

00:30:32.640 --> 00:30:34.635
dine your time at Next.

00:30:34.635 --> 00:30:35.510
Really appreciate it.

00:30:35.510 --> 00:30:36.790
And if you have any questions--

00:30:36.790 --> 00:30:37.640
ROB SCHMIDT: Yeah, we're
open for questions.

00:30:37.640 --> 00:30:40.490
We've got about 15 or so
minutes left, it looks like.

00:30:40.490 --> 00:30:43.130
And we're happy to take any
questions you might have.

00:30:43.130 --> 00:30:45.280
